By Staff Writer ,
3 hours ago
Your chance to beat the crowd for the Aug. 18 primary election and vote early is almost up. So far, more than 1 million Florida residents have taken advantage of the opportunity to show up at designated polls and vote early, according to the News Service of Florida.
The Division of Elections on Wednesday posted that more than 865,000 mail ballots have been returned to county elections offices and nearly 328,000 early votes.
If you prefer to avoid any potential crowds, you can still vote early through the weekend.
In DeSoto County, vote at the Administrative Building, 201 E. Oak St., Arcadia, from 8 a.m.-5 p.m. through Saturday, Aug. 15.
In Charlotte County, there are four areas for early voting 8:30 a.m.-6 p.m. through Saturday, Aug. 15.
Charlotte County locations are:
Centennial Park Recreational Center, 1120 Centennial Blvd., Port Charlotte;
Charlotte Harbor Event and Conference Center, 75 Taylor St., Punta Gorda;
Mac V. Horton West County Annex, 6868 San Casa Dr., Englewood;
Mid-County Regional Library, 2050 Forrest Nelson Blvd., Port Charlotte.
Of the 153,291 eligible voters in Charlotte County, 12,005 have sent in their ballots as of last week — for a 7.83% turnout so far, according to the Charlotte County Supervisor of Elections website. That’s included 5,705 Republicans; 4,098 Democrats; 1,880 No Party Affiliation and 322 “other,” it stated.
In Sarasota County, balloting takes place from 8:30 a.m. until 4:30 p.m. through Sunday, Aug. 16 at:
North Port Elections Office, Biscayne Plaza, 13640 Tamiami Trail;
Venice Elections Office, R.L. Anderson Administration Center, 4000 S. Tamiami Trail;
Sarasota Elections Office Terrace Building, 2001 Adams Lane, Sarasota;
Colonial Oaks Park, 5300 Colonial Oaks Blvd., Sarasota;
Gulf Gate Library, 7112 Curtiss Ave, Sarasota;
North Sarasota Library, 2801 Newtown Blvd., Sarasota.
